==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|matrone}}, from {{der|en|fro|matrone}}, from {{der|en|la|mātrōna||married woman}}, from {{m|la|māter||mother}}. {{doublet|en|matrona}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmeɪtɹən/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-matron.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪtɹən|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[mature]] or [[elderly]] woman.
#* {{RQ:Fuller Church History|passage=grave from her cradle, insomuch that she was a '''matron''' before she was a mother}}
# A wife or a widow, especially, one who has borne children.
# A woman of [[staid]] or [[motherly]] manners.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|act=IV|scene=iii|page=146|column=2|passage=But there’s no bottome, none&nbsp;/ In my Voluptuouſneſſe&nbsp;: Your Wiues, your Daughters,&nbsp;/ Your '''Matrons''', and your Maides, could not fill vp&nbsp;/ The Ceſterne of my Luſt, and my Deſire&nbsp;/ All continent Impediments would ore-beare&nbsp;/ That did oppoſe my will.}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|IX|passage=“A tight little craft,” was Austin’s invariable comment on the '''matron'''; and she looked it, always trim and trig and smooth of surface like a converted yacht cleared for action. &para; Near her wandered her husband, orientally bland, invariably affable,{{nb...}}.}}
# A [[housekeeper]], especially, a woman who manages the domestic economy of a public institution.
# A senior female [[nurse]] in an establishment, especially a hospital or school.
#: {{ux|en|the '''matron''' of a school or hospital}}
# {{lb|en|US}} A [[female]] [[prison officer]].

